  "snippet": "### Niko Niko Park – Cheap Fun with the Kids\n\nTokyo parks get crowded.  On weekends (especially if you have more than one small child) your kids will run off and disappear like ninjas on a moonless night.  Due to the crowds, there also tend to be queues of 6 (\n\n### Two Parks for the Price of One: Inokashira Park Zoo\n\nThe mascot of Inokashira Park Zoo is a squirrel, so to be completely honest, you shouldn’t really be expecting much in terms of animal variety. However, at 400yen for a passport-less adult (with a passport, foreigners get a 20% discount), (\n\nninja rental wifi router japan\n\n### Up to 80% off Travel WiFi from NINJA WiFi\n\n### Challenge Sumo Wrestlers and Enjoy Lunch\n\nThe Hakone Ropeway over a plain of grasses and Mt Fuji in the background [...] Tokyo Cheapo logo\nFamily\n\n# Family\n\nDespite having a population in decline, Tokyo is an amazing place for family activities, with countless adventures both indoors and out, as well as generous ticket reductions for kids.\n\n## Playgrounds and Parks\n\nPlaygrounds are a serious business here, check out these free ones and for a day out consider Niko Niko park which is fenced in and even has a summer beer-garden you can make the most your kids running wild. There’s a free Moomin-themed park in Saitama which is as magical as you would hope.\n\n## Child-friendly Museums and Galleries [...] ### Hanayashiki: Japan’s Oldest Amusement Park\n\nQuick! Name an amusement park in Japan. Tokyo Disney Resort and Universal Studios Japan (USJ) will probably come to mind. But what about we focus on a homegrown Japanese theme park for a change? It’s time to get acquainted with (\n\n### The Best Free Playgrounds in Tokyo\n\nTokyo is a treasure chest of free entertainment for families with small children and far more green and imaginative than you would expect from a concrete jungle. The good news is, most of them are either very free or very (\n\n### Niko Niko Park – Cheap Fun with the Kids"
}{
  "title": "How to Spend 5 Days in Tokyo With Kids - 5 Lost Together",
  "url": "https://www.5losttogether.com/5-days-tokyo-with-kids",
  "snippet": "Our fifth and last day in Tokyo was a short day because we had a flight out that evening.  We felt like we had seen a lot already and decided to treat the kids to some kid-friendly attractions at Odaiba.  My guidebook didn’t mention Odaiba, but it is a shopping and entertainment district on a man-made island in Tokyo Bay that offers many Tokyo family attractions.  You take a monorail to get there, which is a fun experience in itself.  There are a few stops on the island and different shopping/entertainment complexes.  It is a really kid-friendly place and while it may be commercial, if you are looking for what to do with kids in Tokyo, Odaiba should be on your list.  Jason has a great post on 21 things to do in Odaiba with kids which gave us lots of ideas.  We let the kids pick a couple [...] There are some fantastic kid friendly Tokyo tours for families.  Here are a selection of the best-rated Tokyo tours with kids:\n\n Sumo Lesson & Japanese Lunch\n Ghibli Museum and Hayao Miyazaki Movies Tour\n Anime and Manga Culture Tour\n Asakusa Rickshaw Tour\n Tokyo Hop-on Hop-off Bus\n\n## Tokyo with Kids Map\n\nTo help you plan your family trip to Tokyo, I have included a map with all of the sites, attractions, things to do, hotels and restaurants mentioned in this post.\n\n## Theme Parks"
}{
  "title": "Sightseeing Spots for the Family | The Official Tokyo Travel Guide, GO TOKYO",
  "url": "https://www.gotokyo.org/en/story/guide/sightseeing-spots-for-the-family/index.html",
  "snippet": "### Parks and gardens\n\nTokyo's parks and outdoor spaces provide a welcome respite from the urban landscape and relentless pace of the city. You can easily spend a day at places like Ueno Park in eastern Tokyo, which comprises playgrounds, shrines, a lake and the Ueno Zoo. Elegant and traditional gardens like the Hama-rikyu Gardens and Arisugawa Park have ample space for children to explore and wander, while riverside Futakotamagawa, only 15 minutes by train from Shibuya, combines parklands, family-friendly eateries and river banks.\n\n### Only in Tokyo [...] ### Only in Tokyo\n\nFor once-in-a-lifetime and only-in-Tokyo experiences, consider visiting Shinjuku's Ninja Trick House for an intimate chance to learn about these masters of secrecy and espionage. Hands-on but less Japanese is KidZania Tokyo, an indoor theme park where children aged 3-15 are invited to role play adult jobs. Plan your visit for Wednesdays, when English is pretty much the lingua de franca.\n\n### World-class views"
}{
  "title": "The 30+ best things to do in Tokyo with kids (2026) - Globetotting",
  "url": "https://globetotting.com/things-to-do-with-kids-in-tokyo",
  "snippet": "The Global Family to Family community holds close to 100 child-friendly events every month in cities across Japan, including Tokyo, Kyoto and Osaka. Events include picnics in parks, zoo visits, BBQs and more, and they are open to local families and visitors alike and are a great way to meet other families – and get the inside track on the best things to do in Tokyo!\n\nFind out more information on their website.\n\nTokyo with Kids\n\nTake part in a Global Family to Family event in Tokyo\n\n7 day itinerary Tokyo\n\nThe most popular stall at the Tsukiji Fish Market\n\n### Nezu Shrine\n\nNot far from Ueno Park is Nezu Shrine, less well known by tourists but it’s well worth visiting, especially if your family vacation coincides with azalea season. [...] The other reason to visit Ueno Park is for the cherry blossoms. More than 1,000 cherry blossom trees line the park’s central pathway making it a popular destination for hanami, the traditional Japanese art of cherry blossom viewing.\n\n### Meet local families and take part in kid-friendly events\n\nOne of the best ways to get to know a place is by meeting locals. They are the ones who can tell you where you should go for lunch, share top tips on how to snag tickets to the Ghibli Museum and guide you on the best sights and attractions. The good news is that in Tokyo, there is a group of families that can do just this. [...] The crossing has appeared in films including Lost in Translation and The Fast and the Furious: Tokyo Drift as well as numerous music videos, animations and more. Don’t forget to say hello to the small statue of Hachikō once you’ve made it safely across the intersection.\n\n### Miraikan – National Museum of Emerging Science and Innovation\n\nHands down one of the best museums in Tokyo is Miraikan, the National Museum of Emerging Science and Innovation. Located on the manmade island of Odaiba in Tokyo Bay, this family-friendly and interactive museum explores our relationship to science, nature and technology across three different zones. We spent a morning here and had a great time."
}